#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152117 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186151879 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "92a06a13-9e6b-53b1-9fad-ec6cba8b9545" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the LCM of Two Single-Variable Polynomials"}] :question/id #uuid "588a1460-6cd3-519a-ae98-7bc80c7a946c" :question/math-academy-id "e-14809" :question/problem "Find the least common multiple of $9(x-1)^4$ and $12(x-1)^2.$" :question/worked-solution "To find the least common multiple ($\\textrm{LCM}$) of two polynomials that are made up of products of binomials, we find the least common multiple of the constants and the least common multiple of the variable part. Then, we multiply all of the results together.\n\nFirst, we find $\\textrm{LCM}(9,12)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n- Multiples of $9 \\mathbin{:}$ $\\quad 9, \\, 18, \\, 27, \\, {\\color{blue}36}, \\, 45, \\ldots$\n\n- Multiples of $12 \\mathbin{:}$ $\\quad 12, \\, 24, \\, {\\color{blue}36}, \\, 48, \\ldots$\n\nSo, $\\textrm{LCM}(9,12) =  {\\color{blue}{36}}.$\n\nTo find the $\\textrm{LCM}$ of $(x-1)^4$ and $(x-1)^2,$ we raise $(x-1)$ to the largest power that appears in the expressions, which is $4.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\textrm{LCM}\\left((x-1)^4, \\, (x-1)^2\\right) = {\\color{red}{(x-1)^4}}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we multiply all of our results together:\n\n$$\n\\textrm{LCM}\\left(9(x-1)^4, \\, 12(x-1)^2\\right) = {\\color{blue}{36}}\\cdot {\\color{red}{(x-1)^4}} = 36(x-1)^4.\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "92a06a13-9e6b-53b1-9fad-ec6cba8b9545" :knowledge-point/question-generator {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186518639 :question-generator/id #uuid "de32ecbd-6776-5d59-84a1-1380db4ea0f4" :question-generator/script "generate/scratch/17592186152117.py"} :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152516 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "92a06a13-9e6b-53b1-9fad-ec6cba8b9545" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the LCM of Two Single-Variable Polynomials"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/id #uuid "fe98f23f-b63f-5b3b-93d9-9bd2e3e5e383" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186149600}] :question/id #uuid "23d9a941-362d-5b43-b727-77e05cacf8dd" :question/math-academy-id "q-222016" :question/problem "The least common multiple of $9(y + 7)^{4}$ and $3(y + 7)^{2}$ is {{field-1}}."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152744 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "92a06a13-9e6b-53b1-9fad-ec6cba8b9545" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the LCM of Two Single-Variable Polynomials"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "813b9d12-3005-5562-83e3-cad4df49c185" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6(y - 4)^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186150972} {:answer/id #uuid "bdf863f9-99db-5789-b184-4076d8cec3a4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6(y - 4)^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186150973} {:answer/id #uuid "cf4c912f-c08f-5c71-82d9-cc97eb31bdfb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2(y - 4)^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186150974} {:answer/id #uuid "305525ba-5cd5-544c-aa63-b2c499bf7789" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3(y - 4)^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186150975} {:answer/id #uuid "5348a2fd-31a6-5cfe-9567-cf6e85ce552b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3(y - 4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186150976}] :answer-field/id #uuid "e9ab3b75-da54-5fa7-8a97-d807052c826f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186150971}] :question/id #uuid "1d8ab5a8-e076-5cc6-9e2b-5fc7fcef9ac6" :question/math-academy-id "q-76460" :question/problem "Find the least common multiple of $2(y - 4)^{2}$ and $3(y - 4)$."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290353 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "92a06a13-9e6b-53b1-9fad-ec6cba8b9545" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the LCM of Two Single-Variable Polynomials"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ce81a481-fe7e-518c-a1d7-bd34e84fea7f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "14(z+3)^{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289409}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ce81a481-fe7e-518c-a1d7-bd34e84fea7f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "14(z+3)^{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289409} :answer-field/id #uuid "cb6457be-66fa-5a61-8d4b-7978b2734725" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186290173}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "cd9da29a-b393-5138-bdff-e8e16834aa3d" :question/math-academy-id "q-218483" :question/problem "The least common multiple of $7(z+3)^{2}$ and $2(z+3)^{5}$ is{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "To find the least common multiple ($\\text{LCM}$) of two polynomials that are made up of products of binomials, we find the least common multiple of the constants and the least common multiple of the variable part. Then, we multiply all of the results together.\n\nSo first, we find $\\text{LCM}(7,2):$\n\n- Multiples of $7:$ $\\,7,\\,14,\\,28,…$\n\n- Multiples of $2:$ $\\,2,\\,4,\\,6,\\,8,\\,10,\\,12,\\,14,\\,24,…$\n\nSo, $\\text{LCM}(7,2)=14.$\n\nTo find the $\\text{LCM}$ of $(z+3)^{2}$ and $(z+3)^{5}$ we raise $(z+3)$ to the largest power that appears in the expressions, which is $5.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\text{LCM}((z+3)^{2},\\,(z+3)^{5})=(z+3)^{5}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we multiply all of our results together:\n\n$$\n\\text{LCM}(7(z+3)^{2},\\,2(z+3)^{5})=14\\cdot (z+3)^{5}=14(z+3)^{5}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290386 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "92a06a13-9e6b-53b1-9fad-ec6cba8b9545" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the LCM of Two Single-Variable Polynomials"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "020bcddf-6dba-5a6c-bfad-755a7d039e8e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "40(x-1)^{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288041}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "020bcddf-6dba-5a6c-bfad-755a7d039e8e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "40(x-1)^{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288041} :answer-field/id #uuid "a425cf36-1923-5b1e-a442-6afa4a3ee362" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186290100}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "920b8fd7-c6c7-592b-b8ac-58c5083f2e3a" :question/math-academy-id "q-222013" :question/problem "The least common multiple of $5(x-1)^{3}$ and $8(x-1)^{4}$ is{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "To find the least common multiple ($\\text{LCM}$) of two polynomials that are made up of products of binomials, we find the least common multiple of the constants and the least common multiple of the variable part. Then, we multiply all of the results together.\n\nSo first, we find $\\text{LCM}(5,8):$\n\n- Multiples of $5:$ $\\,5,\\,10,\\,15,\\,20,\\,25,\\,30,\\,35,\\,40,…$\n\n- Multiples of $8:$ $\\,8,\\,16,\\,24,\\,32,\\,40,\\,48,\\,56,…$\n\nSo, $\\text{LCM}(5,8)=40.$\n\nTo find the $\\text{LCM}$ of $(x-1)^{3}$ and $(x-1)^{4}$ we raise $(x-1)$ to the largest power that appears in the expressions, which is $4.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\text{LCM}((x-1)^{3},\\,(x-1)^{4})=(x-1)^{4}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we multiply all of our results together:\n\n$$\n\\text{LCM}(5(x-1)^{3},\\,8(x-1)^{4})=40\\cdot (x-1)^{4}=40(x-1)^{4}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290387 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "92a06a13-9e6b-53b1-9fad-ec6cba8b9545" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the LCM of Two Single-Variable Polynomials"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0e2d2fef-25c0-587d-b915-1baa50eebd4d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "30(w+3)^{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288133}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0e2d2fef-25c0-587d-b915-1baa50eebd4d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "30(w+3)^{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288133} :answer-field/id #uuid "710e948a-0db1-5114-aba0-8bf4d460ca33" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289978}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "a3b9782f-5b5f-5eb0-8fa1-1ab86047cc94" :question/math-academy-id "q-222021" :question/problem "The least common multiple of $15(w+3)^{3}$ and $10(w+3)^{6}$ is{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "To find the least common multiple ($\\text{LCM}$) of two polynomials that are made up of products of binomials, we find the least common multiple of the constants and the least common multiple of the variable part. Then, we multiply all of the results together.\n\nSo first, we find $\\text{LCM}(15,10):$\n\n- Multiples of $15:$ $\\,15,\\,30,\\,45,\\,60,…$\n\n- Multiples of $10:$ $\\,10,\\,20,\\,30,\\,40,…$\n\nSo, $\\text{LCM}(15,10)=30.$\n\nTo find the $\\text{LCM}$ of $(w+3)^{3}$ and $(w+3)^{6},$ we raise $(w+3)$ to the largest power that appears in the expressions, which is $6.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\text{LCM}((w+3)^{3},\\,(w+3)^{6})=(w+3)^{6}\n$$\n\nFinally, we multiply all of our results together:\n\n$$\n\\text{LCM}(15(w+3)^{3},\\,10(w+3)^{6})=30\\cdot (w+3)^{6}=30(w+3)^{6}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290388 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "92a06a13-9e6b-53b1-9fad-ec6cba8b9545" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the LCM of Two Single-Variable Polynomials"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "14a0e110-653e-5959-ac4f-8f4f958ca254" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "40(y-2)^{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288173}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "14a0e110-653e-5959-ac4f-8f4f958ca254" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "40(y-2)^{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288173} :answer-field/id #uuid "8051f9f9-9451-50a9-a1e3-eb0f9fd17f95" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186290011}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "b0cd1da4-cfec-56f1-a8d9-6ec40f8f1bec" :question/math-academy-id "q-222022" :question/problem "The least common multiple of $8(y-2)^{9}$ and $20(y-2)^{7}$ is{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "To find the least common multiple ($\\text{LCM}$) of two polynomials that are made up of products of binomials, we find the least common multiple of the constants and the least common multiple of the variable part. Then, we multiply all of the results together.\n\nSo first, we find $\\text{LCM}(8,20):$\n\n- Multiples of $8:$ $\\,8,\\,16,\\,24,\\,32,\\,40,…$\n\n- Multiples of $20:$ $\\,20,\\,40,\\,60,\\,80,…$\n\nSo, $\\text{LCM}(8,20)=40.$\n\nTo find the $\\text{LCM}$ of $(y-2)^{9}$ and $(y-2)^{7},$ we raise $(y-2)$ to the largest power that appears in the expressions, which is $9.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\text{LCM}((y-2)^{9},\\,(y-2)^{7})=(y-2)^{9}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we multiply all of our results together:\n\n$$\n\\text{LCM}(8(y-2)^{9},\\,20(y-2)^{7})=40\\cdot (y-2)^{9}=40(y-2)^{9}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345221 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "92a06a13-9e6b-53b1-9fad-ec6cba8b9545" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the LCM of Two Single-Variable Polynomials"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5d89e619-199e-55ac-8eca-9d77fffa7706" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "30(y+1)^{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343023} {:answer/id #uuid "a00fafbc-22ed-52d6-8f01-31dffa057768" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "30(y+1)^{10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343651} {:answer/id #uuid "da747809-5242-5b07-9971-7285cceb6b5c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.typ