e $\\text{LCM}$ of $(y+4)^{5}$ and $(y+4)^{3},$ we raise $(y+4)$ to the largest power that appears in the expressions, which is $5.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\text{LCM}((y+4)^{5},\\,(y+4)^{3})=(y+4)^{5}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we multiply all of our results together:\n\n$$\n\\text{LCM}(10(y+4)^{5},\\,12(y+4)^{3})=60\\cdot (y+4)^{5}=60(y+4)^{5}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186518584 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "92a06a13-9e6b-53b1-9fad-ec6cba8b9545" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the LCM of Two Single-Variable Polynomials"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2ada290d-9432-53f0-8b43-52b7d855315e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "45(x+8)^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186518589}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2ada290d-9432-53f0-8b43-52b7d855315e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "45(x+8)^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186518589} :answer-field/id #uuid "5f283d16-3b9e-53c7-92f6-b4d170691f6e" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186518608}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "07b1d066-f62b-5882-a508-7ac492a0feff" :question/problem "The least common multiple of $9(x+8)^{3}$ and $5(x+8)$ is {{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "To find the least common multiple ($\\text{LCM}$) of two polynomials that are made up of products of binomials, we find the least common multiple of the constants and the least common multiple of the variable part. Then, we multiply all of the results together.\n\nSo first, we find $\\text{LCM}(9,5):$\n\n- Multiples of $9:$ $\\,9,\\,18,\\,27,\\,36,\\,45,\\,54,…$\n\n- Multiples of $5:$ $\\,5,\\,10,\\,15,\\,20,\\,25,\\,30,\\,35,\\,40,\\,45,\\,50,…$\n\nSo, $\\text{LCM}(9,5)=45.$\n\nTo find the $\\text{LCM}$ of $(x+8)^{3}$ and $(x+8),$ we raise $(x+8)$ to the largest power that appears in the expressions, which is $3.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\text{LCM}((x+8)^{3},\\,(x+8))=(x+8)^{3}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we multiply all of our results together:\n\n$$\n\\text{LCM}(9(x+8)^{3},\\,5(x+8))=45\\cdot (x+8)^{3}=45(x+8)^{3}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186518598 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "92a06a13-9e6b-53b1-9fad-ec6cba8b9545" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the LCM of Two Single-Variable Polynomials"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "088281f1-30f8-5154-9897-fc1de16b9755" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "120" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186518585} {:answer/id #uuid "3a7054ea-8f8c-5f06-b532-76ee11085af1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "120(y-7)^{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186518594} {:answer/id #uuid "3c2bfb7a-d902-5b13-8e20-99df11476124" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "120(y-7)^{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186518595} {:answer/id #uuid "aea5c66a-39d1-5fa4-89bc-20d69071a36b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3(y-7)^{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186518623} {:answer/id #uuid "d3eeff64-1581-5ac7-85d2-75aa7ce7c150" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "120(y-7)^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186518629}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3a7054ea-8f8c-5f06-b532-76ee11085af1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "120(y-7)^{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186518594} :answer-field/id #uuid "4b0cc90f-3f97-5ff6-9d11-248e86764270" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186518602}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "4006d2d6-9185-5769-8637-824db93c6ebf" :question/problem "Find the least common multiple of $24(y-7)^{3}$ and $15(y-7)^{6}.$" :question/worked-solution "To find the least common multiple ($\\text{LCM}$) of two polynomials that are made up of products of binomials, we find the least common multiple of the constants and the least common multiple of the variable part. Then, we multiply all of the results together.\n\nSo first, we find $\\text{LCM}(24,15):$\n\n- Multiples of $24:$ $\\,24,\\,48,\\,72,\\,96,\\,120,\\,144,…$\n\n- Multiples of $15:$ $\\,15,\\,30,\\,45,\\,60,\\,75,\\,90,\\,105,\\,120,\\,135,…$\n\nSo, $\\text{LCM}(24,15)=120.$\n\nTo find the $\\text{LCM}$ of $(y-7)^{3}$ and $(y-7)^{6},$ we raise $(y-7)$ to the largest power that appears in the expressions, which is $6.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\text{LCM}((y-7)^{3},\\,(y-7)^{6})=(y-7)^{6}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we multiply all of our results together:\n\n$$\n\\text{LCM}(24(y-7)^{3},\\,15(y-7)^{6})=120\\cdot (y-7)^{6}=120(y-7)^{6}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186518601 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "92a06a13-9e6b-53b1-9fad-ec6cba8b9545" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the LCM of Two Single-Variable Polynomials"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "79bcda46-ddb9-54aa-890a-6f484ffd64a4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186518612} {:answer/id #uuid "8f197391-3430-5cc9-b457-52570e2ea245" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12(y+2)^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186518615} {:answer/id #uuid "b6883bbb-d3ac-5fe0-a975-1194abc380c4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12(y+2)^{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186518627} {:answer/id #uuid "e09a6cc8-919b-558b-a705-ba8cd8f35773" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2(y+2)^{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186518632} {:answer/id #uuid "e2884404-ab9d-5f48-bd1e-6821f662e726" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12(y+2)^{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186518633}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e2884404-ab9d-5f48-bd1e-6821f662e726" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12(y+2)^{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186518633} :answer-field/id #uuid "ef294fc8-b541-5837-b5de-a407